==English==

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*ḱh₂d-}}
From {{uder|en|fr|récidivisme}}, from {{uder|en|la|recidīvus||returning, recurring}}. Compare {{m|en|recidivous}}, {{af|en|-ism}}. {{surf|en|recidive|-ism}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/rɪˈsɪdɪvɪzm̩/|a=RP}}
* {{IPA|en|/ɹəˈsɪdəvəzm̩/|a=UK,US}}
* {{enPR|r(ĭ)-sĭ'-dĭ-vĭ-zm̩}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-recidivism.wav|a=Southern England}}

===Noun===
{{en-noun|~}}

# Committing new [[offense]]s after a [[crime]] committed in the past.
#: {{ux|en|The increase in criminal activity was attributed to '''recidivism'''.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Sophie Kleber|title=As AI Meets the Reputation Economy, We're All Being Silently Judged|work=w:Harvard Business Review|archiveurl=https://web.archive.org/web/20230528105231/https://hbr.org/2018/01/as-ai-meets-the-reputation-economy-were-all-being-silently-judged|location=Brighton, M.A.|publisher=w:Harvard Business Publishing|date=2018-01-29|issn=0017-8012|oclc=1751795|passage=Unintended mathwashing occurs when the algorithm is left unchecked, and, learning from historical data, amplifies social bias. The U.S. justice system uses an algorithm called COMPAS to determine a criminal's likelihood to re-offend. COMPAS has been proven by Pro Publica to predict that black defendants will have higher rates of '''recidivism''' than they actually do, while white defendants are predicted to have lower rates than they actually do.}}
# {{lb|en|psychology|psychiatry}} {{U|chronic}} [[repetition]] of [[criminal]] or other [[antisocial]] behavior.
# {{lb|en|by extension}} Returning to a negative [[behavior]] after having stopped it for a period of time.
#: {{ux|en|alcohol(ic) '''recidivism'''}}

